    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green (3/3 passing, clean compile).\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling from a 10-digit format to E.164 form.\n\n**New normalization logic** \u2014 `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/Telephones.java`:\n- Strips spaces, dashes and brackets.\n- If a leading `+` is present, keeps it and the country code as supplied.\n- Otherwise assumes country code `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits.\n- Requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`, throwing `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) otherwise.\n- So `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, and `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`.\n\n**Wiring the change in:**\n- `BuildOwner.java` \u2014 replaced the old \"strip non-digits, require exactly 10\" logic with `Telephones.toE164(...)`, so the stored/returned `telephone` is the E.164 string.\n- `EnsureUniqueTelephone` already compares `owner.getTelephone()` against `findByTelephone(...)`, so duplicate detection now naturally compares E.164 values (no change needed there).\n- `Owner.java` entity \u2014 updated the bean-validation constraints from `@Digits`/`@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` to `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")`, and removed the now-unused `@Digits` import. This was the source of the initial 500s: the persistence-layer constraint still demanded 10 bare digits and rejected the `+`.\n\n**Docs kept in sync:**\n- Updated the `InvalidTelephoneException` Javadoc and the `openapi.yml` telephone description to state the E.164 rule.\n\nThe PUT/update flow was left untouched \u2014 it never normalized the telephone previously, so this change is scoped to the create behavior the specification and tests target.",
